cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this repository or its results, please cite the paper below."
title: "Captured weight and boundary leakage bound the error of sample-based spectral functions"
authors:
  - family-names: "Bonilla Vargas"
    given-names: "Nicolás"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-6155-4391"
    affiliation: "Daita AI"
    email: "ngbonillav@unal.edu.co"
abstract: >-
  Sample-based quantum diagonalization is moving from energies to dynamics. Given the subspace the
  shots populated, how wrong is the spectral function built on it? We prove a two-sided bound on the
  relative L1 error of Rayleigh-Ritz reconstruction: with w the captured Born weight of the probe, the
  error is at least 1-w and at most (1+sqrt(w))(sqrt(1-w) + Lambda(eta)/eta), Lambda the normalized
  coupling of H across the subspace boundary at resolution eta, computable from the Ritz vectors in
  hand. Shots buy the first term down, not the second. We retract the weight-only bound of versions 1
  and 2 of this preprint: an analytic counterexample forces its constant to the trivial value. On
  Hubbard sectors up to dimension 731808 the replacement is worse than the trivial bound at every
  published fraction, by factors 2.8 to 4.9, so those reconstructions are uncertified; what survives is
  a calibration, the bound crossing the trivial one at a true relative error of 1.2 to 7.3e-3. No
  orbital-rotation invariant substitutes for it: one-body fermionic magic collapses to
  F1 = 4 tr[gamma(1-gamma)] = 2 Nu, constant along an orbit on which the determinant support runs from
  O(K) to 2^K at bond dimension 2. Validated against exact diagonalization across Hubbard chains and a
  nineteen-molecule suite, and demonstrated on the IBM Heron processor. We claim no quantum advantage.
